{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Cart totals
  Phase 8. The subtotal, any cart-level discounts, and the estimated total.

  Accepts:
  - nothing. It reads the cart object.

  Usage:
  {% render 'cart-totals' %}

  Two consumers — the drawer and the cart page — which is why it is a snippet.
  They were two byte-identical copies under different class names until the
  Phase 8 review pointed out that a correction to one would not reach the
  other.

  THREE LINES, NOT ONE.
  items_subtotal_price is after line-level discounts and before cart-level
  ones, so showing it alone as "the price" silently overstates what the
  customer will pay the moment a cart-level discount is active. Any such
  discount is listed between the two, from Shopify's own applications.

  THE TAX AND SHIPPING LINE IS DERIVED, NOT ASSERTED.
  Whether tax is already in the price is a fact about the store, and Shopify
  knows it: cart.taxes_included. Printing "Taxes and shipping are calculated at
  checkout" unconditionally would be a claim about this merchant's tax
  configuration that nobody supplied, and would be wrong for any tax-inclusive
  market — which the Philippines is.
{%- endcomment -%}

<dl class="cart-totals">
  <div class="cart-totals__row">
    <dt>{{ 'cart.subtotal' | t }}</dt>
    <dd>{{ cart.items_subtotal_price | money }}</dd>
  </div>

  {%- for discount in cart.cart_level_discount_applications -%}
    <div class="cart-totals__row cart-totals__row--discount">
      <dt>{{ discount.title }}</dt>
      <dd>&minus;{{ discount.total_allocated_amount | money }}</dd>
    </div>
  {%- endfor -%}

  <div class="cart-totals__row cart-totals__row--final">
    <dt>{{ 'cart.estimated_total' | t }}</dt>
    <dd data-cart-total>{{ cart.total_price | money }}</dd>
  </div>
</dl>

<p class="cart-totals__note">
  {%- if cart.taxes_included -%}
    {{ 'cart.shipping_at_checkout' | t }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{ 'cart.taxes_at_checkout' | t }}
  {%- endif -%}
</p>
